      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)={9}^{x}-1{x}^{3}+2x-1$, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({9}^{x}-1{x}^{3}+2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({9}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({9}^{x})-1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\  & =\\ln (9)\\cdot {9}^{x}-1(3{x}^{2})+2(1)-0 \\\\  & =\\ln (9)\\cdot {9}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(\\ln (9)\\cdot {9}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+2)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\ln (9)\\cdot {9}^{0}-3(0)^{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\ln (9)\\cdot 1-0+2 \\\\  & =\\ln (9)+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
