      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=1\\cdot {4}^{x}+1\\cdot {6}^{x}-4x^2+2$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1\\cdot {4}^{x}+1\\cdot {6}^{x}-4x^2+2) \\\\ &=1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({4}^{x})+1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x})-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x^2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ &=1({4}^{x}\\ln(4))+1({6}^{x}\\ln(6))-4(2x)+0 \\\\ &=1\\ln(4)\\cdot {4}^{x}+1\\ln(6)\\cdot {6}^{x}-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(1)&=1\\ln(4)\\cdot {4}^{(1)}+1\\ln(6)\\cdot {6}^{(1)}-8(1) \\\\ &=1(4)\\ln(4)+1(6)\\ln(6)-(8)(1) \\\\ &=-8 + 4 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)} + 6 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
